#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
clouds_3d.py  —  3-D satellite cloud animation over the 
==========================================================================
Downloads one local day of geostationary infrared (IR) imagery and renders a
lit, perspective 3-D "cloud-top terrain" animation:

  * cloud-top height is estimated from IR brightness temperature (BT);
    colder tops = taller clouds, so the surface rises with convection
  * ordinary clouds are shaded grey; the coldest, deepest convective tops use
    an enhanced-IR rainbow (blue > cyan > green > yellow > orange > red), so
    big thunderstorm complexes glow red
  * dark sea, thin white GSHHS full-resolution coastlines (every island the
    dataset holds), and city labels for the main islands
  * output: animated GIF (and MP4)

Data sources
------------
  himawari  (default, no account)  Himawari-9 AHI band 13 (10.4 µm), NOAA Open
            Data bucket on AWS. The region sits near the western edge of the
            disk, so pixels are ~5-6 km; still good for convective systems.
  meteosat  Meteosat IODC SEVIRI IR_108 from the EUMETSAT Data Store. Better
            viewing angle; needs a free API key in EUMETSAT_KEY/EUMETSAT_SECRET
            (https://api.eumetsat.int/api-key).

Install
-------
  pip install satpy pyresample s3fs eumdac pyvista numpy scipy pillow \
              imageio imageio-ffmpeg basemap-data-hires

  On a headless Linux server PyVista needs off-screen rendering
  (it usually works out of the box; otherwise `pip install vtk-osmesa`
  or run under `xvfb-run`).

Usage
-----
  python clouds_3d.py                         # yesterday, Himawari
  python clouds_3d.py --date 2026-10-25 --step 10
  python clouds_3d.py --source meteosat
  python clouds_3d.py --demo                  # synthetic, no download
"""

